#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "1f374865-c7f7-510c-b361-fbad352d86c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3452" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve  $\\mathbf r(t) =  t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin t\\, \\mathbf j$ at $t = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {|x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)|} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 0|}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "2ff129e7-a923-5510-a35b-a6be403152ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-3453" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y = x^3$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(x)$ of a plane curve $y = y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(x) = \\dfrac {|y''(x)|} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $y'(x)$ and $y''(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6x|}{{(1+9{x}^{4})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)|}{{(1+9(-1)^{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6|}{{(1+9)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{10}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119579 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "d8e470ad-6a9a-5a03-a90e-b21b24cb6862" :question/math-academy-id "e-3454" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{ (1+\\cos^2 t)^3 } }, \\quad t\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12cda2cf-c551-5d78-829f-a35494c82c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{e}^{t}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278764} {:answer/id #uuid "6c10e1ca-a3bc-5f19-ae45-29d3acc725f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279572} {:answer/id #uuid "a53d1992-e0ca-5333-8795-767dd3d08920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280056} {:answer/id #uuid "abf19c06-71c2-5624-8f7a-bcd51629dbfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280115} {:answer/id #uuid "deea4051-4975-5031-bc42-7c6080a2980e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{(2{e}^{t}+4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abf19c06-71c2-5624-8f7a-bcd51629dbfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280115} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a9c70f9-0e6e-5ad8-959b-dbf96021c602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e34cb96-b8bc-52b1-b1de-e832f51c9600" :question/math-academy-id "q-162388" :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+2t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|{e}^{t}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot {e}^{t}|}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+{2}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2{e}^{t}|}{{({e}^{2t}+4)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268815c7-b490-5e93-a8ee-efe368546e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278935} {:answer/id #uuid "33b4c973-67bd-593d-8ee6-9ffc8b3d97a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279042} {:answer/id #uuid "47270782-87f4-54c1-b4dc-25bf082f89f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279226} {:answer/id #uuid "49dd3ea5-a92d-50f3-8fda-495162dc9acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279253} {:answer/id #uuid "a72fdde5-0412-535d-b4b8-d25187d86251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a72fdde5-0412-535d-b4b8-d25187d86251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280072} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a06755c-1888-5b86-aee2-7b2eb3388583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "394c8fec-2d21-5f70-acbd-8f636bfaf360" :question/math-academy-id "q-162397" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\,1-t⟩$ at $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3},\\,y(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =3{t}^{2},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =6t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|3{t}^{2}\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 6t|}{{((3t)^{2}+(1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6|t|}{{(9{t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{6|1|}{{(9(1)^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{(10)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{1}{κ(1)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d7d11c-5e09-580d-9138-b2bcbdadc1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279201} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1f84b4-0821-51d6-89b0-fead6e75723e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279865} {:answer/id #uuid "abca34d9-3dbe-5d7a-8d86-11d35949a569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280113} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe657e7-03d8-5b7e-922e-2f4b3a890faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280279} {:answer/id #uuid "f43f243d-8c70-54f6-9400-9bf0fe9a5dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abca34d9-3dbe-5d7a-8d86-11d35949a569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280113} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b1d5e59-f911-53c6-a483-5c396f9856e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b29a950e-ff6a-53c6-9c95-7f20926ddb5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162404" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+(1-t)\\,j$ at $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,y(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2t\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2|}{{((2t)^{2}+(-1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{{(4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "514f5bcf-ca33-5ef0-a733-ec86dffae285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444904} {:answer/id #uuid "8538bef5-2a64-541e-8605-5f22bd698faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{(t+1)^{3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444909} {:answer/id #uuid "b649dd5a-85ff-5b4b-adbd-8d96f4510c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{2}+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444916} {:answer/id #uuid "da852d6f-b131-56d4-8256-fdebdbc61a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{(t+1)^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "514f5bcf-ca33-5ef0-a733-ec86dffae285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444904} :answer-field/id #uuid "227607dc-c995-56fe-a20c-664bdc03dd83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41bae79a-9c3e-5b74-b3cc-213f841b749e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162415" :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2(t+1)^{3}}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}},\\,t>-1.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot \\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}-0\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}})|}{{({1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}})}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}|}{{(1+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{4}})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}|}{\\sqrt{{[\\frac{(t+1)^{4}+1}{(t+1)^{4}}]}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{6}}{|(t+1)^{3}|\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{6}}{(t+1)^{3}\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{3}}{\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because $t>-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037e0218-2ccb-5fc5-a7d9-90d6fd723b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+{e}^{-t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444898} {:answer/id #uuid "3478a58b-9f79-5e34-a211-77269420ac07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444902} {:answer/id #uuid "4d339910-47cb-5c30-93e5-6488d858b18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444903} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a98d71-c58f-5d5f-a189-b5539ed24ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444913} {:answer/id #uuid "b42b656a-892d-51db-80a8-a81810a225fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({e}^{t}+{e}^{-t})^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3478a58b-9f79-5e34-a211-77269420ac07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444902} :answer-field/id #uuid "d781f999-1594-5949-8737-c9a323ba0c9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62dfa070-b095-529f-9742-123438c631da" :question/math-academy-id "q-162416" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{2}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot {e}^{t}}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+({e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57815f7b-af61-528d-a997-36687ced0737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401352} {:answer/id #uuid "d4db65b8-6f9c-5494-a06b-92bcdce809b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401353} {:answer/id #uuid "eb263125-4fe2-540b-a807-c4ea79034c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401354} {:answer/id #uuid "9709336c-60b5-5d73-aab9-35c162acf334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401355} {:answer/id #uuid "7440cb05-c5ac-59cb-a23c-02e409faf53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9709336c-60b5-5d73-aab9-35c162acf334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401355} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0797b50-1acd-58f0-8079-590c6e07a834" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac9e9607-a2dd-5594-94f3-d6c7ba9a234a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162418" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-{t}^{2}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{2\\cdot (\\,\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(t)=-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2)-(-2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c29e5d-b1a7-5613-96fd-eb90123329c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444900} {:answer/id #uuid "18321e2d-03ab-5b7f-b6cf-eb257b7975d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444901} {:answer/id #uuid "76ff0fbe-7e33-5c49-a981-0cf35543aabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444907} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd01b5f-502e-5b84-8ab6-aef88da96da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444908} {:answer/id #uuid "8fab1247-6445-5427-bfd4-c38eeb5e52b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16c29e5d-b1a7-5613-96fd-eb90123329c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444900} :answer-field/id #uuid "13db80f1-1ccc-58f1-8641-3684ab9f70ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a0a2c2f-1706-5f21-a00b-714f5830e8bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-162419" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨1-2t,\\,{e}^{2t}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{(1+{e}^{4t})^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-2t,\\,y(t)={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-2,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{-2\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}-0\\cdot 2{e}^{2t}}{{((-2)^{2}+(2{e}^{2t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8{e}^{2t}}{{(4+4{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8{e}^{2t}}{8{(1+{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{(1+{e}^{4t})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c476023-3edd-5454-ad4c-d620372551a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444899} {:answer/id #uuid "5b451fb0-b40b-5c70-b25e-e6d23ad872c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444905} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d40450-bdc6-5d3a-b702-e8236d07b93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444918} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b8ff58-1e96-5cc3-b54c-7bc562e920f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444919} {:answer/id #uuid "f0de606c-6689-5321-a410-2a40373d9b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c476023-3edd-5454-ad4c-d620372551a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444899} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dbf3265-830d-57eb-a3a8-4ed3e1aae9cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37e058b7-fa62-5f7d-a443-fba67e4c6b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162422" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨(1-t)^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1-t)^{2},\\,y(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-2(1-t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|-2(1-t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 2|}{({(-2(1-t))^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2t{e}^{t}-4{e}^{t}|}{({(2(1-t))^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{|2(0){e}^{(0)}-4{e}^{(0)}|}{({(2(1-(0)))^{2}+({e}^{(0)})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4166d235-26fd-55c2-ba75-a545a0c6714c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279175} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4f5558-c7b3-50d9-9d22-de8fab0fd956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279275} {:answer/id #uuid "4d57d1fb-25b1-5c73-89a3-d36dba6a22dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279287} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4e5861-a325-58b9-b231-448c50cb7791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279588} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9e25be-40ab-5eca-8a7c-df19ac612398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c4f5558-c7b3-50d9-9d22-de8fab0fd956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279275} :answer-field/id #uuid "14e1b3a4-df1f-517d-a217-9d6a9ba2488c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7c93d46-b6af-5493-bffe-c7cad1a03b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-162476" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))|}{{((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-8{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-8{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)|}{{(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d9ba14f-0d41-5eb4-ad9c-a3ca3c24f04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444906} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8c1238-64a6-5834-b740-28291402e0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444910} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd14e87-92c1-5247-b76f-922de0ae6d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444912} {:answer/id #uuid "af28de7d-ae99-5116-bc25-465dc14a4553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444914} {:answer/id #uuid "e634d627-dd7c-5af7-b31a-020ab478d655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e634d627-dd7c-5af7-b31a-020ab478d655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444921} :answer-field/id #uuid "b69e114a-e3b9-5a0f-8073-62abdcb55456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e3e184e-e905-5d9a-a39c-5d0db9ad9647" :question/math-academy-id "q-162524" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{{[1+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{|-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi |}{{[1+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1-0|}{{[1+(0+1)^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a45b223-ee35-5b3b-8b23-b0fc15afd7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278826} {:answer/id #uuid "505702d7-1b83-5ad9-923e-c38e7ed28816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279327} {:answer/id #uuid "84710217-2579-56fb-9e4c-10ffa0b06735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279780} {:answer/id #uuid "cc55dd62-bdf4-53b1-8a83-33b727ec650d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280385} {:answer/id #uuid "f91d910c-5425-5446-a517-ce4132dad6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84710217-2579-56fb-9e4c-10ffa0b06735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279780} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fbb904c-13a8-58c8-af31-bf7bd27b63ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cd5ce7b-a731-529f-9a0d-e737d8086206" :question/math-academy-id "q-162525" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-4x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x-4|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2}-4x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)-4|}{{(1+{(3(-1)^{2}-4(-1))}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10|}{{(1+{7}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{(50)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{50\\sqrt{50}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4025b0-c98d-57d8-b9f1-7966a6ea1e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278689} {:answer/id #uuid "8a962df1-9f93-5afe-a61f-3514fd82bd9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279829} {:answer/id #uuid "9b62a923-cab5-5d17-ba95-d864663893e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279968} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f27822-8ead-5038-a405-29bb7ee648ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280588} {:answer/id #uuid "e8497efc-f3da-5d9d-9d7d-669b989a38e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b62a923-cab5-5d17-ba95-d864663893e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279968} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4872fea-8e16-51bb-91b0-c19a7fd44213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef4a3d99-d223-509e-9ad1-4a5b346a7377" :question/math-academy-id "q-162526" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{{[1+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0|}{{(1+0)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c7a7e9-da7d-5c85-866c-b2fe189ab9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278912} {:answer/id #uuid "3af6f758-92f1-5104-b867-e764a1444773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279116} {:answer/id #uuid "96ae20c4-d2ba-5df1-9ef7-ee8dda62071d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279925} {:answer/id #uuid "d5735b14-23fc-5b32-8069-3b4c3988f129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280475} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd0405b-e35c-5d85-8bc3-a5a6eedb7d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23c7a7e9-da7d-5c85-866c-b2fe189ab9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278912} :answer-field/id #uuid "b447dd2e-4a24-53d5-85f0-852828cc0edf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d2059e5f-93a8-5b5f-ab4e-bc10c8876989" :question/math-academy-id "q-162534" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y={2}^{x}$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{y}^{″}(x)={2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{[1+{({2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2x}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-1) & =\\frac{{2}^{-1}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2(-1)}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{2})}{{(\\frac{4+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04645806-e767-51f6-b56b-a967a3dc35eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317584} {:answer/id #uuid "16fca06f-ad2b-5ab8-8589-6844750db1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317638} {:answer/id #uuid "815819ce-d705-5379-ba5c-0d922afcf192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318002} {:answer/id #uuid "8bad3fd1-ca56-5eea-b1ef-c4edb5db52bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318034} {:answer/id #uuid "8f73c15c-c406-513b-871a-da9696850ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04645806-e767-51f6-b56b-a967a3dc35eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317584} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6368194-6360-5320-9e2f-ba4aa8d2e3e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08113b99-b301-5e33-b007-837ba481f7b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-34549" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{x-1},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}|}{{[1+{(\\frac{2}{x-1})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(x-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(2-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc2a5bf-fc50-5bdf-a0af-5f1a4575ac15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118335} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6b22ad-4809-5bb5-bd45-2c29a5f05f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118337} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8bdbbb-355b-54d4-a806-644ad3ceeeff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118338} {:answer/id #uuid "76f203ed-87c5-59b7-8132-51c8335abec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118339} {:answer/id #uuid "bc367fc5-9c04-507f-a154-cf7c0d7dcc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+e)^{3/2}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dc2a5bf-fc50-5bdf-a0af-5f1a4575ac15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118335} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1d80a0c-4431-532d-bdec-1a04c311fcf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a657ab49-8316-5498-aff1-401071c8812e" :question/math-academy-id "q-34956" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=(1-t)\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-t,\\,y(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot {e}^{t}-({e}^{t})\\cdot 0|}{{((-1)^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{(1+{e}^{2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{(1+{e}^{2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
